    "answer": "Flask route decorators are just Python decorators: a decorator is a function that takes the function to be decorated as an argument and returns either the same function or a new object. [S4]\n\nHow decorators are applied in Python:\n\n- You place decorator(s) immediately before a function definition (one per line). [S4]\n- The shorthand `@A` `@B` `@C` above a function `def f(): ...` is equivalent to calling them around the function as `f = A(B(C(f)))`. [S4]\n- A decorator function can also take arguments: in that case, if you write `@C(args)` it becomes ` _deco = C(args); f = A(B(_deco(f)))`. [S2]\n\nWhat’s missing: the provided documentation passages do not describe Flask specifically (e.g., which Flask decorator to use for defining routes, or its arguments/behavior). I don't know based on the available Python documentation.",
